<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<!--
	Who may see and change what in E-Home (haumacher/ehome #48): the roles, the groups that hold them,
	and the grants on the ehome.* modules. Administrators (root) bypass all of it.

	Two roles, both global (held on the security root and passed on to every E-Home object):
	- ehome.Installer: the installer who builds and maintains KNX installations. Reads and edits the
	  projects with everything in them (buildings, group addresses, devices, functions), the shared
	  master data (KNX master data, datapoint types, manufacturers with their catalogs and application
	  programs, the Zigbee catalog of known devices), imports ETS projects and product data, programs devices on the bus, registers
	  controllers, sets their tokens and deploys runtime images to them.
	- ehome.Viewer: reads the projects, the master data and the controllers, changes nothing and
	  neither programs devices nor deploys.

	An account holds a role through its membership in the group of the same purpose, "ehome.installers"
	or "ehome.viewers"; an administrator adds the account to the group (Administration, Access control,
	Groups or Accounts).

	The roles reach the objects in two steps (access-configuration article):
	- The groups hold their roles on the security root (singleton rules), and the seven types whose
	  objects no composition holds name the security root as their role parent: the projects, the
	  controllers and the five singletons of the shared master data (manufacturers, datapoint types,
	  mask versions, the Zigbee catalog, the device templates). These seven types decide by the grants
	  below.
	- Every other E-Home type is held in a composition of one of them and delegates the whole decision
	  to its container (the default access parent of a composition part): a device, a group address, a
	  function or a deployment is read where its project is read and changed where the project is
	  written; a catalog entry or an application program where the manufacturers are; a Zigbee
	  device type with its function entries where the Zigbee catalog is; a device template with its
	  object overlays where the device templates are.

	The bearer token of a controller (ehome.runtime:Controller#token) is never readable through the
	model: its read grant lists no role, which denies the read for every user but an administrator.
	An installer sets it (the write follows the controller); the deployment reads it in Java
	(de.haumacher.ehome.runtime.Deployer), which the model access check does not cover. The views show
	only whether a token is set. The secret of an enrolled controller (ehome.runtime:Controller#secret,
	haumacher/ehome #54) is handled the same way: E-Home generates it at the enrollment and checks the
	controller's authentication with it in Java (de.haumacher.ehome.connection); nobody enters or reads
	it. The private installation keys of a project (ehome.project:Project#installationKey and
	#newInstallationKey, homecontrol issue #35) are handled the same way: E-Home generates them and signs
	the runtime images with them in Java (de.haumacher.ehome.connection.InstallationKeys); the views show
	only the fingerprints of the public keys. The open enrollment code of a project (ehome.project:Project#enrollmentCode) is read by
	installers only: whoever knows it can enroll a controller for the project.

	The access tokens of the agent access (module tl-layout-view-agent, tl.agent:AccessToken,
	doc/agent-access.md) are their owner's: an account holds ehome.TokenOwner on the tokens it issued
	(a role rule along AccessToken#owner) and reads and withdraws them in the dialog "Agent access" of
	the development menu; nobody else sees them but an administrator, who lists all tokens in the
	administration. The token is issued and checked in Java (IssueAccessTokenAction, AgentAccess), which
	the model access check does not cover; only the hash of its secret is stored. The two transient
	types of the dialog (the form of a new token and the invitation shown once) are never persisted and
	are marked internal.
-->
<application config:interface="com.top_logic.basic.config.ApplicationConfig$Config"
	xmlns:config="http://www.top-logic.com/ns/config/6.0"
>
	<services>
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.base.services.InitialRolesManager">
			<instance>
				<roles>
					<role name="ehome.Installer"/>
					<role name="ehome.Viewer"/>
					<role name="ehome.TokenOwner"/>
				</roles>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<config service-class="com.top_logic.base.services.InitialGroupManager">
			<instance>
				<groups>
					<group name="ehome.installers"/>
					<group name="ehome.viewers"/>
				</groups>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tool.boundsec.manager.AccessManager">
			<instance>
				<role-rules>
					<rules>
						<singleton-rule id="ehomeInstallersOnRoot"
							role="ehome.Installer"
							target="SecurityStructure#ROOT"
						>
							<group-with-name name="ehome.installers"/>
						</singleton-rule>
						<singleton-rule id="ehomeViewersOnRoot"
							role="ehome.Viewer"
							target="SecurityStructure#ROOT"
						>
							<group-with-name name="ehome.viewers"/>
						</singleton-rule>
						<rule id="ehomeOwnerOfAccessToken"
							meta-element="tl.agent:AccessToken"
							role="ehome.TokenOwner"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.agent:AccessToken#owner"
									inverse="false"
								/>
							</path>
						</rule>
					</rules>
				</role-rules>
				<security-parents>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForProject"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.project:Project"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForController"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.runtime:Controller"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForManufacturers"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.catalog:ManufacturerRoot"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForDatapointTypes"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.datapoint:DatapointTypes"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForMaskVersions"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.operational:MaskVersions"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForZigbeeCatalog"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.zigbee.catalog:ZigbeeCatalog"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<rule id="ehomeRootForDeviceTemplates"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="ehome.template:DeviceTemplates"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
					<!-- The values of the enumerations, see the grant of tl.model:TLClassifier below (TL #30275). -->
					<rule id="ehomeRootForClassifier"
						inherit="true"
						meta-element="tl.model:TLClassifier"
					>
						<singleton module="SecurityStructure"/>
					</rule>
				</security-parents>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<config service-class="com.top_logic.model.security.SecurityConfigurationService">
			<instance>
				<security-config>
					<!--
						The modules of six of the seven types deciding for themselves; the seventh, the root
						of the mask versions, has grants of its own below. The types of the other modules
						(ehome.building, ehome.logical, ehome.physical, ehome.operational, ehome.monitor,
						ehome.zigbee) are composition parts and delegate to their containers.
					-->
					<module name="ehome.project">
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Read" roles="ehome.Installer, ehome.Viewer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Write" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</module>
					<module name="ehome.runtime">
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Read" roles="ehome.Installer, ehome.Viewer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Write" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</module>
					<module name="ehome.catalog">
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Read" roles="ehome.Installer, ehome.Viewer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Write" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</module>
					<module name="ehome.datapoint">
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Read" roles="ehome.Installer, ehome.Viewer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Write" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</module>
					<!-- The root of the shared mask versions, the one type of ehome.operational no composition holds. -->
					<class name="ehome.operational:MaskVersions">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="ehome.Installer, ehome.Viewer"/>
						<grant operation="Write" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</class>
					<module name="ehome.zigbee.catalog">
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Read" roles="ehome.Installer, ehome.Viewer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Write" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</module>
					<!-- The device templates (haumacher/ehome #103): the singleton decides, its templates and their overlays are composition parts. -->
					<module name="ehome.template">
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Read" roles="ehome.Installer, ehome.Viewer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Write" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Create" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
						<grant inherit="true" operation="Delete" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</module>
					<!--
						The security root stands for E-Home as a whole: the commands that read no object of
						the model (the searches for KNX gateways and controllers on the network) check Read
						there (haumacher/ehome #65), which both E-Home roles hold, an account without one of
						them not. The root has no grants of its type; the operation Administration declared
						for it by the engine stays with the administrators.
					-->
					<singleton name="SecurityStructure#ROOT">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="ehome.Installer, ehome.Viewer"/>
					</singleton>
					<!-- A read grant without roles: nobody but an administrator reads the token. -->
					<part name="ehome.runtime:Controller#token">
						<grant operation="Read"/>
					</part>
					<!-- The same for the secret of an enrolled controller (haumacher/ehome #54). -->
					<part name="ehome.runtime:Controller#secret">
						<grant operation="Read"/>
					</part>
					<!--
						The private installation keys of a project (homecontrol issue #35): nobody but an
						administrator reads them; E-Home signs with them in Java.
					-->
					<part name="ehome.project:Project#installationKey">
						<grant operation="Read"/>
					</part>
					<part name="ehome.project:Project#newInstallationKey">
						<grant operation="Read"/>
					</part>
					<!-- An agent access token: its owner reads and withdraws it. -->
					<class name="tl.agent:AccessToken">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="ehome.TokenOwner"/>
						<grant operation="Delete" roles="ehome.TokenOwner"/>
					</class>
					<class name="tl.agent:NewAccessToken" internal="true"/>
					<class name="tl.agent:IssuedAccessToken" internal="true"/>
					<!--
						The AI modules (haumacher/ehome #126, #129). Their conversations, memories and personal
						access tokens are their owner's by the policies the modules ship
						(tl-ai-core.chatSecurity.config.xml, tl-ai-core.accessTokens.config.xml). The types
						below are read and written by the modules' code and by administrators only: the form of
						a new personal access token (transient, never persisted), the agents and skills authored
						in the running application (E-Home's agents are configured, administrators author
						further ones), the pages an agent publishes (the group "workspace", granted to nobody),
						and the period of the agent monitor of the administration (transient).
					-->
					<class name="tl.ai.access:AccessTokenRequest" internal="true"/>
					<class name="tl.ai.core:Agent" internal="true"/>
					<class name="tl.ai.core:Skill" internal="true"/>
					<class name="tl.ai.htmlpages:GeneratedHtmlPage" internal="true"/>
					<class name="tl.ai.monitor:Period" internal="true"/>
					<!--
						The values of the enumerations (a schedule's weekdays, the kind of a function, ...) are
						model data both roles read, or the MCP tools leave them out of every object a user reads
						(haumacher/ehome #141). Workaround of TL #30275: the engine decides the read of a
						classifier by roles instead of treating it as model data (doc/tl-todo.md).
					-->
					<class name="tl.model:TLClassifier">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="ehome.Installer, ehome.Viewer"/>
					</class>
					<!-- The open enrollment code of a project: installers only, a viewer must not enroll a controller. -->
					<part name="ehome.project:Project#enrollmentCode">
						<grant operation="Read" roles="ehome.Installer"/>
					</part>
				</security-config>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			The assistant (haumacher/ehome #126, doc/ai.md): which agents, models and tools a run of the
			chat may use, by the roles held on the security root (ai/security/tool-access-configuration,
			ai/security/run-access). What a tool may then read or change is decided per object by the
			grants above, so a viewer's read tools see what the viewer sees, and an installer's write
			tools change what the installer may change - always after the user approved the call.

			- ehome.Installer: reading the model and the data (model.read, data.read) and changing data
			  with the object tools (data.write: updateObject, createObject, deleteObject, editText).
			- ehome.Viewer: reading only.
			- Not granted to anybody: changing data with a TL-Script (script, changeData). E-Home's
			  TL-Script functions are not access checked and include writing to the bus (monitorSend),
			  which no approval of a data change should cover; E-Home's own tools (haumacher/ehome #127,
			  #128) check the project and join data.read and data.write. Equally egress (the web),
			  workspace, and the privileged groups (model.structure, agent.authoring).
			- Every other account: nothing beyond the implicit grants (asking the user, loading a skill),
			  and no agent at all.

			The roles run the chat's agent (TL_SingleAgent) and no other; the model is the operator's
			choice through tl_anthropic_models and tl_ai_default_model, so every model a provider offers
			may be picked.

			A run without anybody to approve its calls - a call of an external agent over the MCP endpoint
			(ehome.mcp.config.xml, haumacher/ehome #129) - is capped by the background allowlist. It admits
			changing data: an installer's MCP client (Claude Code) changes data as the installer could in
			the chat, and the client asks its user before a call the tool list marks destructive. The
			roles still decide: a viewer's client is refused every writing tool. E-Home starts no agent
			on a schedule.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tl.ai.core.security.AiToolAccessService">
			<instance>
				<tool-access>
					<role name="ehome.Installer">
						<grants>
							<grant group="model.read"/>
							<grant group="data.read"/>
							<grant group="data.write"/>
						</grants>
					</role>
					<role name="ehome.Viewer">
						<grants>
							<grant group="model.read"/>
							<grant group="data.read"/>
						</grants>
					</role>
				</tool-access>
				<baseline/>
				<background-allowlist>
					<grant group="model.read"/>
					<grant group="data.read"/>
					<grant group="data.write"/>
				</background-allowlist>
				<agent-access default="none">
					<role name="ehome.Installer">
						<grants>
							<grant agents="TL_SingleAgent"/>
						</grants>
					</role>
					<role name="ehome.Viewer">
						<grants>
							<grant agents="TL_SingleAgent"/>
						</grants>
					</role>
				</agent-access>
				<model-access default="all"/>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			The check covers the E-Home modules only: the modules of the engine have no complete access
			definition of their own (8.0.0-alpha11), and E-Home neither shows nor grants their types to
			its users (see doc/tl-todo.md, "Access coverage of engine modules").
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.element.boundsec.manager.coverage.SecurityCoverageCheck">
			<instance excluded-modules="Contacts,SearchExpressionStructure,SecurityStructure,Tag,mandatorStructure,orgStructure,tl.accounts,tl.comments,tl.contact.ui,tl.core,tl.folder,tl.model,tl.reporting,tl.search,tl.units"/>
		</config>
	</services>
</application>
